  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Tunisian urban working-class dance genre named for the mezwed bagpipe, formed in the shanty neighbourhoods of Tunis in the 1960s-70s and popularized on cassette by Hedi Habbouba, Salah Farzit and Samir Loussif — the double-reed bagpipe carrying a continuous ornamented drone-melody over bendir and darbuka playing the fast 6/8 fezzani and 4/4 saidi patterns at 120-150 BPM, with rough colloquial-Tunisian vocals about poverty, prison and exile and dense audience handclapping.",
